†Order Parts L56:   * cite41†Ratio Flex Program L57: \nL58: cite42†Select Region L59: \nL60: cite18†Media cite42†Change Region L61: \nL62:   * cite1†Products L63: ### Category\nL64: \nL65:     * cite2†Axle L66:     * cite3†Driveshaft L67:     * cite4†Transmission L68:     * cite5†Lubricants L69: \nL70: ### Application\nL71: \nL72:     * cite6†Automotive L73:     * cite7†Performance L74:     * cite8†Commercial L75: ### Specialty\nL76: \nL77:     * cite9†Jeep_{®} L78:     * cite10†Ford L79:     * cite11†Spicer Select^{®} L80:     * cite12†GM HD L81:     * cite13†Dodge^{®}/Ram^{®} L82: \nL83:     * cite25†What's the Diff? L84:     * cite43†Click here to go to our e-catalog†danaaftermarket.com L85: \nL86: [Input: Find parts by search]\nL87: \nL88:   * cite1†Applications L89: \nL90:     * cite33†Automotive L91:     * cite34†Performance L92:     * cite35†Commercial L93:     * cite44†Crate Axle L94:     * cite45†Builder Axle L95: \nL96:   * cite1†Resources L97:     * cite14†How to Use DanaAftermarket.com L98:     * cite15†Event/Vehicle Build Support Requests L99:     * cite16†Dana Training Academy™ L100:     * cite17†Upgrade and Off-Road Education L101:     * cite18†Media L102:     * cite19†Events L103:     * cite20†Horsepower Wars L104:     * cite21†Articles L105:     * cite22†Calculators L106:     * cite23†Videos L107:     * cite24†Dana Vehicle Builds L108:     * cite26†Anglemaster L109:     * cite27†Lube & Torque Specifications L110:     * cite28†Downspeeding Solutions L111:     * cite29†Measuring U-Joints L112:     * cite30†Merchandise L113:     * cite31†Seal Installation Tool Kit L114:   * cite32†Literature†media.spicerparts.com L115:   * cite36†Where to Buy L116:   * # Driveline Operating Angle Calculator\nL117: \nL118: Share\nL119: \nL120: cite46†Literature cite36†Where To Buy L121: ## Literature\nL122: \nL123: Use the Driveline Operating Angle Calculator to find the operating angle of each u-joint in your drivetrain.\nL124: \nL125: cite47†iframe L126: \nL127: Measure the angles of each component in your drivetrain—driving member, driveshaft(s), driven member—to find your u-joint operating angles.\nL128: \nL129: cite48†Read More... L130: \nL131: Measure the angles of each component in your drivetrain—driving member, driveshaft(s), driven member—to find your u-joint operating angles.\nL132: A universal joint operating angle is the angle that occurs between the driving member and driveshaft, and between the driven member and driveshaft when they are not vertically aligned.\nL133: \nL134: NOTE: This calculator does not address compound drive angles (horizontal offsets).\nL135: \nL136: You’ll need a spirit level or digital protractor accurate to ¼ degree. You’ll be measuring angles at various points:\nL137: \nL138:   * From the transmission\nL139:   * Along the drivelines\nL140:   * Back to the rear axles\nL141: Enter the angle to the nearest ¼ degree, along with the slope (up or down):\nL142: \nL143:   * Up: Rises from front to rear of vehicle\nL144:   * Down: Descends from front to rear\nL145: \nL146: Three ways to measure:\nL147: \nL148:   * Use an adapter for a digital protractor that allows for measurement of slopes directly off bearing caps in light duty or quick disconnect applications\nL149:   * Remove bearing cap and take measurement directly off yoke\nL150:   * Remove snap ring and take measurement off bearing cap using adapter\nL151: \nL152: Measurement tips:\nL153:   * Measure along actual center line of output shaft\nL154:   * Measure on flat surface 90 degrees to or parallel to output shaft of transmission\nL155:   * Measure true center line\nL156: \nL157: Basic rules:\nL158:   * Universal joint operating angles at each end of a driveshaft should always be at least one-half degree\nL159:   * Universal joint operating angles on each end of a driveshaft should always be equal within one degree of each other (one half degree for motor homes and shafts in front of transfer cases or auxiliary devices)\nL160:   * For vibration-free performance, universal joint operating angles should not be larger than three degrees. If they are, make sure they do not exceed the maximum recommended angles.\nL161: Driveshaft RPM  | Max. Operating Angle  | Interaxle\nL162: --- | --- | ---\nL163: Parallel  | Intersecting\nL164: 5000  | 3.2°  | —  | —\nL165: 4500  | 3.7°  | —  | —\nL166: 4000  | 4.2°  | —  | —\nL167: 3500  | 5.0°  | —  | —\nL168: 3000  | 5.8°  | —  | —\nL169: 2500  | 7.0°  | —  | —\nL170: 2000  | 8.7°  | —  | —\nL171: 1500  | 11.5°  | —  | —\nL172: Points to remember:\nL173: \nL174:   * The angles shown on the above chart are the maximum u-joint operating angles recommended by Spicer engineers and are directly related to the speed of the driveshaft.\nL175:   * Any universal joint operating angle greater than 3 degrees will lower universal joint life and may cause a vibration.\nL176:   * Remember to check maximum safe driveshaft RPM by using the cite49†Critical Speed RPM Calculator .\nL177: \nL178: cite50†Less... L179: \nL180: How to use this calculator:\nL181: \nL182: cite51†iframe†www.youtube.com L183: cite52†iframe†www.youtube.com L184: \nL185: Find a cite36†Spicer parts distributor today , or find where you can place your order now on Dana’s streamlined eCommerce platform, cite53†DanaAftermarket.com†www.danaaftermarket.com .\nL186: These tools provide mathematical calculations only. The instructional videos are intended only to provide guidance on how the calculators operate and are illustrative only. Neither the calculators nor the associated videos are intended to provide advice or information on vehicle operation, maintenance or safety. Dana makes no representation as to what equipment or use is appropriate for any specific vehicle.\nL187: ## Related Parts\nL188: \nL189: cite54†Commercial Driveshaft Assemblies L190: